    "hook": {
      "word": "HOOK",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A piece of metal, or other hard material, formed or bent into a curve or at an angle, for catching, holding, or sustaining anything; as, a hook for catching fish; a hook for fastening a gate; a boat hook, etc.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "That part of a hinge which is fixed to a post, and on which a door or gate hangs and turns.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An implement for cutting grass or grain; a sickle; an instrument for cutting or lopping; a billhook. Like slashing Bentley with his desperate hook. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Steam Engin.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "See Eccentric, and V-hook.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A snare; a trap. [R.] Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A field sown two years in succession. [Prov. Eng.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "pl.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The projecting points of the thigh bones of cattle; -- called also hook bones. By hook or by crook, one way or other; by any means, direct or indirect. Milton. \"In hope her to attain by hook or crook.\" Spenser. evening, by water, to the Duke of Albemarle, whom I found mightly off the hooks that the ships are not gone out of the river.\" Pepys. one's self. [Colloq. U.S.] Bartlett. which the forward part of the deck rests. together the opposite edges of a garment, etc. suspended, as from the top of a wall. hooks. instead of suckers, as in the genera Enoploteuthis and Onychteuthis. instead of a jaw, for turning a bolthead, nut, or coupling.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To catch or fasten with a hook or hooks; to seize, capture, or hold, as with a hook, esp. with a disguised or baited hook; hence, to secure by allurement or artifice; to entrap; to catch; as, to hook a dress; to hook a trout. Hook him, my poor dear, . . . at any sacrifice. W. Collins.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To seize or pierce with the points of the horns, as cattle in attacking enemies; to gore.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To steal. [Colloq. Eng. & U.S.] To hook on, to fasten or attach by, or as by, hook.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To bend; to curve as a hook.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
